Hard fork of bb 1.3rc1, the AA-project demo (https://aa-project.sourceforge.net/bb/)
Upstream tarball: bb-1.3rc1.tar.gz  sha256: 793d88c872793b6dab444cf5bab24f283ecb2f3502f1479ebbe41dd8e90b81c4
Frozen: no upstream merges. License: GPL-2.0 (see COPYING — kept, and AUTHORS
with it: the demo's own credits scroll is the authors' work and stays).

The tree that upstream ships is 81 files; this carries the 42 sources and 16
headers the binary is actually built from, the three .s3m tracks, and nothing
else. What went: the whole autotools apparatus (configure, Makefile.in,
aclocal.m4, install-sh, missing, mkinstalldirs, acconfig.h, aconfig.h.in,
stamp-h.in), which is replaced by a static src/aconfig.h and a build.sh that
calls the compiler; and ChangeLog, NEWS, INSTALL, bb.lsm, README.LZO.

KDOS changes, all applied in place because this is our source now:

  main.c    the mixer runs on its own thread. Upstream pumped MikMod_Update
            from a 10ms timer in the same group bbwait() pumps, and bbwait()'s
            caller has just blocked in aa_flush() writing the frame to the
            terminal — so under a compositor the audio starved whenever the
            window was actually being drawn, and was perfect when it was
            minimised.
  tex.c     clear_zbuff() cleared sizeof(long) per cell against a sizeof(int)
            allocation. The same size in 1997, double on x86_64: the heap
            corrupts and stage 2 aborts on "malloc(): corrupted top size".
  messager.c the scroll used memcpy on overlapping ranges. glibc survived it;
            musl is free not to. memmove.
  config.h  REGISTERS(n) no longer expands to __attribute__((regparm(n))),
            which is x86-32 only and warns on every declaration here.

Then the rebranding, which is deliberate rather than incidental. The demo is
the AA-group's and the credits scroll, the greetings and the history stay
theirs; what changed is everything that would now be FALSE, plus the fork's
own mark in the two beats that already spend time on a logo.

  bb.c      the arguments are parsed as a list, and -nosound and -mixer join
            -loop. Usage names kdos-bb.
  main.c    THE DEMO STARTS BY ITSELF. Upstream asked "Music?[Y/n]" and then
            held the mixer settings table until Continue was pressed -- two
            questions whose answers were always yes and default, in front of
            the one thing the program does. Both are flags now.
            song_progress() is new: thousandths through the module, which is
            what paces the extro scroll.
  scene1.c  blazinec()'s nineteen flash words are reworded and the mascot is
            drawn in the twelfth, which upstream spent on an empty string.
            THE COUNT IS THE CLOCK -- one strobe pair each -- so the scene is
            the same length to the microsecond and the music stays in step.
  credits.c the same rule: text[] is 166 entries and CSIZE drives TOTAL, so
            entries are reworded and never added. Every contributor line and
            every Special Thanks is untouched; the fork's own credit takes
            the four slots after the last contributor, and the pointer that
            named README now names this file, which is the one that exists.
  credits2.c the mascot in the extro's logo beat, and THE CLOSING TEXT
            SCROLLS ITSELF. It was a pager that waited on a keypress, so the
            demo ended by sitting still until somebody noticed. It now asks
            the player where it is and places the text at the same fraction
            of its own length, landing on the last page just before bb3.s3m
            ends. Any key hands control back for good -- a scroll that pulled
            the page out from under a reader would be worse than none.
            The module LOOPS (update_sound restarts it), so a fraction that
            went backwards is read as "the song is over".
  textform.c the closing document gains what KDOS is and what this fork
            changed, and loses the claims that were no longer true.
  kdostux.c kdos.png as an LZO-compressed greyscale image in the format
            image.h describes, so dispimg() draws it with the same scaling,
            dithering and strobe as the photographs. GENERATED -- see
            genimg.py, which is host-only, checks its own round trip, and is
            never run by the build or on the target.
